-- ---- type: exhibit (From the Aurora) --------------------------
insert into public.museum_content (key, value) values ('exhibit:aurora-01', '{"type":"exhibit","area":"aurora","order":0,"narration":"data:audio/mpeg;base64,","data":{"year":"1928","file":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","aspect":0.8508,"paper":"The Aurora","city":"","date":"1928","page":"","metaHTML":"<b>The Aurora</b><br/>1928","headline":"Expansion of Our Sorority","deck":"","excerpt":"Expansion of Our Sorority\n\nBy Delight Hinton\n\nMrs. Delight Hinton\n\nMODERN business methods can be successfully used in planning the expansion of our sorority. The editor of this publication has suggested that it sell Sigma Gamma Rho first to us, then to the public. We must believe in our organization, respect it and love it before we invite others to share our sisterhood.\n\nA strong spirit of mutual and individual regard must be fostered by our individual chapters. We do not advocate selfishness, but we do advocate that a sister deserves favors, sympathy and consideration over any outsider.\n\nThe national meetings can be made to promote a feeling of unity, for there contacts are made and respect for the accomplishments of the various chapters and their localities are established. Sectional meetings which will naturally evolve as we expand will clear the atmosphere of good fellowship.\n\nWe expect The Aurora to be a potent factor in welding us solidly. With a copy of it in the hands of every builder and every soror we can see the tangible results of six years'' work in pictures, articles, social items, and what not.\n\nWhen each member has become thoroughly imbued with the spirit of true friendship and the supremacy of Sigma Gamma Rho''s motives and ideals then, and not until then, shall we be ready to carry out a national expansion program.\n\nThis can be done by letting the public know who we are and what we are doing, through contributions to local and national publications. These press notices should tell of the work of the local chapters and the grand chapter. The reading public has respect for the organizations which are forging ahead, blazing trails and setting standards.\n\nWhen this information has been adequately disseminated we shall have fertile fields for new chapters. The national and district organizers can work out definite plans concerning the number of chapters desired, their location, the number and type of members to be taken in. There can be no lowering of our standards or close adherence to any one profession. Our aim shall be to take in only the highest type of women in each community and all professions.\n\nTo epitomize this article: First, we must secure one hundred per cent cooperation and loyalty in our present group; next, we must sell our organization to the public; third, the work of expansion must be developed by strong organizers with definite plans","excerptLabel":"From the article","scene":"Delight Hinton lays out a 1928 plan for the sorority''s expansion: sell Sigma Gamma Rho to its own members first, then to the public, through press notices and strong organizers, with no lowering of standards.","thread":"","threadLabel":"","source":"article","line":"Delight Hinton''s 1928 plan for the sorority''s national expansion.","alt":"The Aurora, 1928: “Expansion of Our Sorority” by Delight Hinton, with her portrait"}}'::jsonb)
on conflict (key) do update set value = excluded.value, updated_at = now();

insert into public.museum_content (key, value) values ('exhibit:aurora-02', '{"type":"exhibit","area":"aurora","order":1,"narration":"data:audio/mpeg;base64,","data":{"year":"1928","file":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","aspect":1.3766,"paper":"The Aurora","city":"","date":"1928","page":"","metaHTML":"<b>The Aurora</b><br/>1928","headline":"A Close Up of the Official Family","deck":"","excerpt":"A Close up of the Official Family\n\nMrs. F. J. Leeke — Grand Financial Scriptia\n\nMiss M. Merriwether — Grand Vice Praesidia\n\nMiss Vivian Howard — Grand Recording Scriptia\n\nMrs. Blanch Edwards — Grand Pecunia\n\nMrs. F. O''Bannon — Grand Praesidia\n\nMrs. Gertrude Murchison — Editor-in-Chief\n\nMiss Urnestine Bell — National Organizer","excerptLabel":"From the page","scene":"The Aurora''s portrait gallery of the sorority''s national officers for 1928, under the heading “A Close up of the Official Family.”","thread":"The officers'' titles stand in their early Latin forms, as printed.","threadLabel":"As printed","source":"article","line":"The sorority''s 1928 national officers, in a portrait gallery.","alt":"The Aurora, 1928: “A Close up of the Official Family,” portraits of the sorority''s seven national officers"}}'::jsonb)
on conflict (key) do update set value = excluded.value, updated_at = now();

insert into public.museum_content (key, value) values ('exhibit:aurora-03', '{"type":"exhibit","area":"aurora","order":2,"narration":"data:audio/mpeg;base64,","data":{"year":"1946","file":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","aspect":0.7726,"paper":"Los Angeles Tribune","city":"Los Angeles, California","date":"Saturday, September 7, 1946","page":"Page 10","metaHTML":"<b>Los Angeles Tribune</b> · Los Angeles, California<br/>Saturday, September 7, 1946 · Page 10","headline":"Elected Grand Epistolean","deck":"","excerpt":"CONGRATULATIONS—to Mrs. Dorothy Jones, a delegate to Sigma Gamma Rho sorority''s annual boule, held in Baltimore, Maryland last week. Mrs. Jones was elected to the office of Grand Epistolean, and Sigma Sigma graduate chapter in Los Angeles is proud of her.","excerptLabel":"From the caption","scene":"The Tribune congratulates Mrs. Dorothy Jones of Los Angeles, elected Grand Epistolean at the sorority''s annual boule in Baltimore. Sigma Sigma graduate chapter is proud of her.","thread":"“Grand Epistolean,” as the Tribune prints the office.","threadLabel":"As printed","source":"article","line":"Mrs. Dorothy Jones of Los Angeles, elected Grand Epistolean at the Baltimore boule.","alt":"Los Angeles Tribune, September 7, 1946: portrait of Mrs. Dorothy Jones with the caption congratulating her election as Grand Epistolean"}}'::jsonb)
on conflict (key) do update set value = excluded.value, updated_at = now();

insert into public.museum_content (key, value) values ('exhibit:aurora-04', '{"type":"exhibit","area":"aurora","order":3,"narration":"data:audio/mpeg;base64,","data":{"year":"1952","file":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","aspect":1.3514,"paper":"The Aurora","city":"Phoenix, Arizona","date":"1952","page":"","metaHTML":"<b>The Aurora</b> · Phoenix, Arizona<br/>1952","headline":"Beta Nu Sigma at Their Scholarship Tea","deck":"","excerpt":"Sorors of Beta Nu Sigma Chapter and a group of Patrons at their Scholarship Tea. The Social-Ettes are wearing their flower—the white carnation. Standing, left to right: Ethel Hairston, Robbie Harris, Edith Harris Larrayne Sullivan, Myrtle Anderson, Soror Lavenia Jenkins, Soror Zelma Perkins, Soror Mary Nelson, Soror Ella Mae Tookes, Paunice Dozell, Raydell Johnson, Soror Pearl Meshack and Soror Pat Reynolds. Seated, left, Soror Pearl Bringham, basileus. Seated right, Clemmie Harper, President of the Sigma Social-Ettes.","excerptLabel":"From the caption","scene":"Sorors of Beta Nu Sigma Chapter and a group of patrons at their Scholarship Tea in Phoenix. The Social-Ettes wear their flower, the white carnation.","thread":"","threadLabel":"","source":"article","line":"Beta Nu Sigma''s Scholarship Tea in Phoenix, with the Sigma Social-Ettes.","alt":"The Aurora, 1952: Beta Nu Sigma Chapter and patrons at their Scholarship Tea, Phoenix"}}'::jsonb)
on conflict (key) do update set value = excluded.value, updated_at = now();

insert into public.museum_content (key, value) values ('exhibit:aurora-05', '{"type":"exhibit","area":"aurora","order":4,"narration":"data:audio/mpeg;base64,","data":{"year":"1952","file":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","aspect":1.5723,"paper":"The Aurora","city":"Los Angeles, California","date":"1952","page":"","metaHTML":"<b>The Aurora</b> · Los Angeles, California<br/>1952","headline":"Hostesses for the Grand Basileus","deck":"","excerpt":"Sigma SCoror in Los Angeles, California at thereception given to welcome the Grand Basileus.","excerptLabel":"From the caption","scene":"Sigma Sigma sorors in Los Angeles, at the reception given to welcome the Grand Basileus.","thread":"“Sigma SCoror” and “thereception,” as the caption prints them.","threadLabel":"As printed","source":"article","line":"Sigma Sigma hostesses at the Los Angeles reception welcoming the Grand Basileus.","alt":"The Aurora, 1952: Sigma Sigma hostesses at the reception welcoming the Grand Basileus, Los Angeles"}}'::jsonb)
on conflict (key) do update set value = excluded.value, updated_at = now();

insert into public.museum_content (key, value) values ('exhibit:aurora-06', '{"type":"exhibit","area":"aurora","order":5,"narration":"data:audio/mpeg;base64,","data":{"year":"1952","file":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","aspect":0.7773,"paper":"The Aurora","city":"Los Angeles, California","date":"September 1952","page":"Pages 20–21","metaHTML":"<b>The Aurora</b> · Los Angeles, California<br/>September 1952","headline":"First Far Western Regional Conference Held in Los Angeles","deck":"","excerpt":"Sigma Sigma Chapter was hostess to the first Far Western Regional Conference which was held in Los Angeles July 26-27, 1952. Soror Lessie B. Crossland, Regional Director, presided over the sessions held in the beautiful lounge of the campus YWCA of the University of Southern California.\n\nThe business sessions included a worship service, chapter reports, and a Sorority workshop in which the policies and program were organized for the region. An address by the Grand Basileus, Sallie N. Edwards, presented the history of the sorority and fraternity movement in the United States and its fifty year development within the eight groups which make up the National Pan Hellenic Council. She challenged the delegates to make fuller use of sorority membership for greater service to community life, to aid in the establishment of high scholastic and educational standards, and to work toward the attainment of human rights for all the peoples of the world.\n\nA beautiful Memorial Service directed by Soror Claudia Whitmore paid tribute to two Sigma Sigma sorors — Louise DeBoss and Mattie Brown—who passed on during the year.\n\nThe highlight of the educational program was an address by Dr. Tanner Duckery, Assistant Superintendent of Schools in Philadelphia, Pennsylvania. Dr. Duckery was serving as visiting lecturer in the Intercultural Workshop being held at USC. His message on the work needed in the area of human relations and the progress now being made was illuminating and provocative. His beautiful illustration on the need for a “spiritual vantage point” in every life left the sorors challenged and inspired.\n\nThe social affairs of the conference included a delightful luncheon at the Ontre Cafeteria, a closed banquet in the Wedgewood Room at Carl''s, and a “sip and chat” held at the attractive home of Soror Priscilla Ramos. Several brunches and dinners were given by members of the hostess chapter for visiting sorors.\n\nShown are some of the delegates in attendance at the first Far Western Regional Conference in July.\n\nRegional officers elected for the year were: Grammateus, Pearl Meshack of San Diego; Anti-Grammateus, Irma Simpson of Denver, Colorado; Tamiochus, Myrtle Neal of Phoenix, Arizona; Financial Secretary, Thelma Vick of Oakland, California; Epistoleus, Pollyanna Reid of Portland, Oregon; Parliamentarian, Reatha Herndon of Alpha Nu, the campus chapter in Los Angeles; and Reporter, Virgie Mason, Basileus of Sigma Sigma Chapter. Soror Lesslie B. Crossland was nominated for Regional Syntaktes.\n\nThe 1953 conference will be held in Phoenix, Arizona, the second weekend in July with the Beta Mu Sigma Chapter as hostess.\n\nAlthough two “neighborhood meetings” had been held by the Far Western sorors, this meeting marked the fulfilment of a dream begun with the organization of Sigma Sigma chapter thirteen years ago — July 30, 1939. Recognition must be given to some of those persons whose tireless efforts through the years and at the time of organization made this epochal making step for Sigma possible. There is Soror Rubye G. Watts, Editor-in-Chief of the Aurora, who organized the chapter while she was serving the Sorority as Grand Syntaktes. Soror Bertha Black Rhode, Past Grand Basileus, gave tireless effort to following the contacts which made the chapter possible. Ethel Ross Smith, Past Grand Basileus, gave much time to the work of the program in the Los Angeles chapters. There is also Soror Lessie B. Crossland whose tireless spirit of determination in making contacts and organizing chapters has been largely responsible for the achievement of regional establishment. The charter members of Sigma Sigma Chapter, sorors who transferred to the region from other sections of the country, and the present Grand Basileus, Sallie N. Edwards have all worked together to strengthen the ties of sisterhood on the West Coast and raise the “beacon light” of Sigma high.\n\nDr. Tanner Duckery, Assistant Superintendent of Schools of Philadelphia, Pennsylvania, Soror Lessie Crossland, Regional Syntaktes and Soror Sallie Edwards, Grand Basileus are seen during a brief rest period at the Far Western Regional Conference.\n\nSoror Willie Ruth Davis, Beta Gamma Chapter.\n\nSoror Willie Ruth Davis, basileus of Beta Gamma chapter, is a member of the Pan Hellenic Council, Y.W.C.A., Girl Scouts, Elementary Education Club and a member of East Trinity Methodist Church in Houston. She is the daughter of Mr. and Mrs. Cicero Davis, 4821 Oats Street, Houston, Texas. She graduated from Wiley in August, 1952 and plans to study for her Master''s Degree at the University of Wisconsin.\n\nSoror Delores Chandler, Beta Gamma Chapter.\n\nSoror Delores L. Chandler of Beta Gamma chapter is a member of the Pan Hellenic Council, Y.W.C.A., Girl Scouts, Dramatic Club, Elementary Education Club and of East Trinity Methodist Church in Houston, Texas. She is the daughter of Mrs. Ollie Mae Chandler of Houston. Soror Chandler is majoring in elementary education and will finish her work in December, 1953. She plans to study for her Master''s Degree at the University of Texas, Austin, Texas. She was a delegate from the Elementary Education Club to attend their state meeting in Philadelphia during April, 1952.","excerptLabel":"From The Aurora · September 1952","scene":"Chapter News: Sigma Sigma Chapter hosts the first Far Western Regional Conference, Los Angeles, July 26–27, 1952.","thread":"The report prints the Regional Director''s name as Lessie B. Crossland, and once as Lesslie.","threadLabel":"As printed","source":"article","line":"Sigma Sigma Chapter was hostess to the first Far Western Regional Conference, held in Los Angeles July 26-27, 1952.","alt":"The Aurora, September 1952, page 20: Chapter News — First Far Western Regional Conference Held in Los Angeles, with a photograph of delegates in attendance","img2":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","alt2":"The Aurora, September 1952, page 21: the conference report continued, with Dr. Tanner Duckery, Soror Lessie Crossland and Grand Basileus Sallie N. Edwards pictured during a brief rest period","cap2":""}}'::jsonb)
on conflict (key) do update set value = excluded.value, updated_at = now();

insert into public.museum_content (key, value) values ('exhibit:aurora-07', '{"type":"exhibit","area":"aurora","order":6,"narration":"data:audio/mpeg;base64,","data":{"year":"1955","file":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","aspect":0.7782,"paper":"The Aurora","city":"","date":"September 1955","page":"Page 3","metaHTML":"<b>The Aurora</b><br/>September 1955 · Page 3","headline":"An Open Letter from the Grand Basileus","deck":"","excerpt":"An Open Letter . . . . . . from the GRAND BASILEUS\n\nSoror Edna Douglas, Grand Basileus\n\nDear Sorors:\n\nAfter spending a very delightful summer and the crispness of autumn is in the air, our thoughts turn to a new year in the program of Sigma Gamma Rho Sorority. Let us begin now while enthusiasm is high and ideas are fresh from a wonderful vacation. Ideas mean little unless we implement them. Please offer your suggestions to your program chairman or send them to proper committee chairmen or to me. We can assure you that they will be given due consideration and used if at all possible.\n\nWe have received numerous greetings from Sigma women while they were enjoying the full range of summer pleasures—studying, visiting, camping and travel. Soror Regina Nance was among those who travelled extensively. We received our greeting which was postmarked Rome, Italy. Soror Nance will have to tell us more about this wonderful trip.\n\nSince my last letter to you, I have enjoyed many pleasant moments with Sigma women from the Atlantic to the Pacific. In mid-May, it was my privilege to represent you at the Joint-meeting of Northeast Region of Sigma Gamma Rho and the National Pan Hellenic Council. They were held respectively at Newport News and Hampton Institute, Virginia. Both meetings were well-planned and executed. You will be able to read detailed reports of both elsewhere in this issue. Newport New was at her best as the hostess chapter. My time was too divided to really enjoy the many N. E. Sigmas whom I had known previously.\n\nFrom May 29-June 9, I served in varied capacities on the West Coast. My first opportunity for service was with the Far Western Regional Conference of Sigma Gamma Rho Sorority which was our part of the First Joint Regional Convention of National Pan Hellenic Council-Western Region. The Los Angeles Sigmas had worked very hard to make this Regional meeting representative of our best and they did just that. Everyone enjoyed the program and activities—a job well done by a group of fine Sigma Women.\n\nI next served in the Pan Hellenic Workshop as a Resource Person and appeared on the Joint Public Program.\n\nMaking full use of a geographical advantage, I visited Beta Theta Sigma, Oakland; Gamma Alpha Sigma, Bakersfield and\n\n(Continued on page 11)","excerptLabel":"From the letter","scene":"Grand Basileus Edna Douglas opens the new sorority year, writing of her May 29–June 9, 1955 service on the West Coast: the Far Western Regional Conference in Los Angeles, the Pan Hellenic Workshop, and visits to Beta Theta Sigma in Oakland and Gamma Alpha Sigma in Bakersfield.","thread":"","threadLabel":"","source":"article","line":"Grand Basileus Edna Douglas''s open letter opening the new sorority year, September 1955.","alt":"The Aurora, September 1955, page 3: “An Open Letter from the Grand Basileus,” Edna Douglas, with her portrait"}}'::jsonb)
on conflict (key) do update set value = excluded.value, updated_at = now();

insert into public.museum_content (key, value) values ('exhibit:aurora-08', '{"type":"exhibit","area":"aurora","order":7,"narration":"data:audio/mpeg;base64,","data":{"year":"1955","file":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","aspect":1.3937,"paper":"The Aurora","city":"San Francisco, California","date":"September 1955","page":"","metaHTML":"<b>The Aurora</b> · San Francisco, California<br/>September 1955","headline":"Gamma Zeta Sigma Is Organized in San Francisco","deck":"","excerpt":"Gamma Zeta Sigma — San Francisco, California\n\nOn July 1, 1955, at the beautiful home of soror Hattie Washington, 1475 Golden Gate Avenue in the city of San Francisco, a long wish of the Bay area sorors was culminated with the organization of Gamma Zeta Sigma graduate chapter by Soror Lessie Crossland, Regional Syntaktes. The members of the new chapter are Sorors Lulu Carter, Basileus, Ina Bennett Starghill, Anti-Basileus, Hattie Washington, Grammateus, Ella Tyler, Anti Grammateus, Gertrude Gumbs, Tamiochus, Elizabeth Redus, Epistoleus.\n\nSoror Evelyn Draper, Campus Chapter Coordinator, directed the election of officers, with installation of officers by Soror Crossland. Members of Beta Theta Sigma on hand to welcome the new chapter were Sorors Sims, Steadman, Johnson, Wilkes, and other sorors. Congratulations to Soror Carter, Basileus, who received an M.A. degree in June.\n\nHow recently have you attended a Regional Conference?\n\nWhy not plan to be present at the next gathering of sorors in your region?","excerptLabel":"From the article","scene":"Gamma Zeta Sigma graduate chapter was organized on July 1, 1955, at the San Francisco home of Soror Hattie Washington, by Regional Syntaktes Lessie Crossland. The chapter''s first officers are named.","thread":"This article prints “Lessie Crossland” and “Ella Tyler.”","threadLabel":"As printed","source":"article","line":"Gamma Zeta Sigma graduate chapter organized in San Francisco, July 1, 1955.","alt":"The Aurora, September 1955: “Gamma Zeta Sigma — San Francisco, California,” the chapter''s organization article"}}'::jsonb)
on conflict (key) do update set value = excluded.value, updated_at = now();

insert into public.museum_content (key, value) values ('exhibit:aurora-09', '{"type":"exhibit","area":"aurora","order":8,"narration":"data:audio/mpeg;base64,","data":{"year":"1955","file":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","aspect":1.3062,"paper":"The Aurora","city":"San Francisco, California","date":"September 1955","page":"","metaHTML":"<b>The Aurora</b> · San Francisco, California<br/>September 1955","headline":"Sorors of Gamma Zeta Sigma Chapter, San Francisco","deck":"","excerpt":"Sorors of Gamma Zeta Sigma Chapter . . . San Francisco, California\n\nLeft to right: Sorors Elizabeth Redus, Epistoleus; Lulu Carter, Basileus; Gertrude Gumbs, Tamiochus; Ina B. Starghill, Anti-Basileus; and Hattie Washington, Grammateus. Not in picture, Soror Ella Tyle, Anti-Grammateus.","excerptLabel":"From the caption","scene":"The officers of Gamma Zeta Sigma Chapter, San Francisco, in the chapter''s chartering photograph.","thread":"The caption prints “Ella Tyle.”","threadLabel":"As printed","source":"article","line":"The Gamma Zeta Sigma officers in the chapter''s chartering photograph.","alt":"The Aurora, September 1955: chartering photograph of the Gamma Zeta Sigma officers, San Francisco"}}'::jsonb)
on conflict (key) do update set value = excluded.value, updated_at = now();

insert into public.museum_content (key, value) values ('exhibit:aurora-10', '{"type":"exhibit","area":"aurora","order":9,"narration":"data:audio/mpeg;base64,","data":{"year":"1966","file":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","aspect":0.7861,"paper":"The Aurora","city":"Indianapolis, Indiana","date":"Summer 1966","page":"Page 5","metaHTML":"<b>The Aurora</b> · Indianapolis, Indiana<br/>Summer 1966 · Page 5","headline":"A Beacon Light Retires","deck":"","excerpt":"A Beacon Light Retires\n\nSoror Nannie Mae Gahn Johnson, one of the Founders and Charter Members of Sigma Gamma Rho Sorority, Inc., retires from the Indianapolis public schools this June after 43 years in the field of education in Indianapolis.\n\nSoror Johnson is Principal of School No. 37 and a product of the same school system to which she gave her service. She attended Elementary Schools 42 and 17 and graduated from Shortridge High School. She graduated from the Indianapolis Normal School and received the B.S. and M.S. Degrees from Butler University. Soror Johnson has done additional work on the graduate level at Indiana University and Western Reserve University in Cleveland.\n\nHer basic understanding of the educational needs of young people has come through a close relationship with children, first as a classroom teacher; secondly as an administrator. She has taught at the elementary level at School 23 and School 26. From 1948 until 1956 she served as Assistant Principal of School 26. For the past ten years she has been Principal of the Hazel Hart Hendricks School No. 37.\n\nHer long association with education in Indianapolis has drawn her actively into numerous educational organizations at the local, state, national, and international level. Among these are:\n\nIndianapolis Association of Elementary Principals\n\nIndianapolis Council of Administrative Women in Education\n\nIndianapolis Council of Exceptional Children\n\nIndianapolis Council of Parent-Teacher Association\n\nIndianapolis Education Association\n\nIndianapolis Grade Teachers Association\n\nEnglish Teachers Club of Indianapolis\n\nAssociation for Intensified Education\n\nNational Education Association\n\nSoror Mrs. Nannie Mae Johnson — Principal, Hazel Hart Hendricks School No. 37\n\nSoror Johnson is a member of the Allen Chapel A. M. E. Church. She is active in the Richard Allen Guild of Allen Chapel and has served the church in many other capacities.\n\nSoror Johnson is serving the community as a member of the Flanner House Board of Directors. “Flanner House is an agency dedicated to the basic philosophy of helping people to help themselves.” She is giving her best efforts to greater service for greater progress.\n\nThe Faculty of School 37 will entertain Mr. and Mrs. Johnson at a Retirement Dinner, Wednesday, June 1st. The Parent-Teacher Association of School 37 will hold an open Reception in her honor on Sunday, June 5, in the School Auditorium.\n\nAccolade of the era was given by the Teachers'' Affairs Committee of Indianapolis —\n\n“The retirement of Mrs. Johnson will leave the Indianapolis School System with a void which will be difficult to fill. Those of us who have worked closely with her and the thousands and thousands of children whose lives she has touched will be forever grateful for her guidance through the years.\n\n“Sigma Gamma Rho can be proud of Mrs. Johnson and the role she has played in her chosen field.”","excerptLabel":"From the article","scene":"Founder Nannie Mae Gahn Johnson retires from the Indianapolis public schools in June 1966, after 43 years in education there, the last ten as Principal of the Hazel Hart Hendricks School No. 37.","thread":"","threadLabel":"","source":"article","line":"Founder Nannie Mae Gahn Johnson retires from the Indianapolis public schools.","alt":"The Aurora, Summer 1966, page 5: “A Beacon Light Retires,” Founder Nannie Mae Gahn Johnson, with her portrait"}}'::jsonb)
on conflict (key) do update set value = excluded.value, updated_at = now();

insert into public.museum_content (key, value) values ('exhibit:aurora-11', '{"type":"exhibit","area":"aurora","order":10,"narration":"data:audio/mpeg;base64,","data":{"year":"1966","file":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","aspect":0.7896,"paper":"The Aurora","city":"Los Angeles, California","date":"Summer 1966","page":"Page 12","metaHTML":"<b>The Aurora</b> · Los Angeles, California<br/>Summer 1966 · Page 12","headline":"The 14th Regional Conference, Los Angeles","deck":"","excerpt":"REGIONAL CONFERENCE — Los Angeles, California — June 24-25, 1966\n\nSoror Frankie Johnson — Regional Hostess\n\nSoror Virginia Sims — Western Syntaktes\n\n14th Regional Conference\n\nBiltmore Hotel\n\n“The West''s Largest and Friendliest Hotel”\n\n515 S. Olive St.\n\nLos Angeles, California\n\nOur Sorors down Los Angeles way are hard at work planning and preparing for the biggest and best Regional Conference in the history of Western Region.\n\nSoror Johnson and her committee have set the wheels in motion to roll forward the days to June 24-25, 1966. Along with the business of the sorority, will be hours of entertainment. They hope you will make it a family trip. Entertainment for all age levels.\n\nTentative Schedule\n\nFriday, June 24, 1966\n\n3:00-6:00 p.m.—Pre-registration\n\nExecutive Board Meeting\n\nPress Conference\n\n7:30 p.m.—Reception\n\nSaturday, June 25, 1966\n\n8:30-9:00 a.m.—Late Registration\n\n9:00-12:15 p.m.—First Business Session\n\n12:15-1:30 p.m.—Regional Picture Lunch\n\n1:30-5:00 p.m.—Second Business Session\n\nWorkshop\n\nPanel Discussion\n\n7:00-9:00 p.m.—Banquet\n\n10:00-1:00 p.m.—Presentation Ball\n\nA Tip to the Basileus\n\nBy Soror Ann Stedman\n\nHAVE YOU EVER TRIED —\n\nTo please every member in your Sorority?\n\nTo conduct a meeting every month when the sorors don''t show up?\n\nTo give each Soror some responsibility even though it is not accepted?\n\nTo lead when only a few will follow?\n\nTo love when others hate?\n\nTo step forward and be pushed back?\n\nTo smile when some are frowning?\n\nTo ignore those that constantly stir up confusion?\n\nIF NOT, then, You do not know what is means to be a Basileus.\n\nYOU, WHO ARE BASILEI, be sure to include the following phrases in every agenda,—conduct a meeting each month, give each soror some responsibility, love, build up, step forward, smile, and ignore because you want to continue to build Finer Womanhood.\n\nCalifornia Sigmas congratulate the Barristers in Florida:\n\nNatalie Baskin\n\nMiriam Beckerman\n\nSoror Gwendolyn Cherry\n\nHarriet L. French\n\nCarole Gardiner\n\nRosemary Jones\n\nRhea Pincus\n\nGail E. Pollack\n\nand extend best wishes to each of you.\n\nAppreciably yours,\n\nOllie V. Greene","excerptLabel":"From the page","scene":"The announcement of the 14th Regional Conference, at the Biltmore Hotel in Los Angeles, June 24–25, 1966, with the tentative schedule, Regional Hostess Frankie Johnson, and Western Syntaktes Virginia Sims.","thread":"“what is means to be a Basileus,” as Soror Stedman''s piece prints it.","threadLabel":"As printed","source":"article","line":"The announcement of the 14th Regional Conference, Biltmore Hotel, Los Angeles, June 24–25, 1966.","alt":"The Aurora, Summer 1966, page 12: announcement of the 14th Regional Conference, Los Angeles"}}'::jsonb)
on conflict (key) do update set value = excluded.value, updated_at = now();

insert into public.museum_content (key, value) values ('exhibit:aurora-12', '{"type":"exhibit","area":"aurora","order":11,"narration":"data:audio/mpeg;base64,","data":{"year":"1966","file":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","aspect":0.7861,"paper":"The Aurora","city":"Los Angeles, California","date":"Summer 1966","page":"Page 13","metaHTML":"<b>The Aurora</b> · Los Angeles, California<br/>Summer 1966 · Page 13","headline":"Sigma Sigma — Los Angeles, California","deck":"","excerpt":"Adult education (seated, Soror Catherine L. Stiefel)\n\nAdult Education in Los Angeles\n\n1966 marks the 78th year of adult education in our city. In 1872 the first class was established in Los Angeles; its roots are deeply imbedded in the past. It owes much to its European ancestry.\n\nBy far the great majority of these students are men and women of serious purpose.\n\nThis may be said of this class whose dynamic teacher had to take a leave and during this interim Soror Catherine Stiefel guided them to the end of the term.\n\nSoror Stiefel (teacher) had this to say:\n\n“I feel gratified to serve at Manual Arts School. It is gratifying to see so many thousands of students attending classes from 6:30 to 9:30 p.m. Among the alumni I saw listed the names of former Governor Goodwin Knight, Lawrence Tibbett, and our Soror Ollie V. Greene.”\n\nScholarship Image — Tribute to My Sorors\n\nby Soror Catherine Stiefel\n\nOur local chapter is a little more than 25 years old. We have given scholarships on the average of $300 per year to total $7500.\n\nWe assisted the Rose Bowl princess, helping her to be placed second, turning in a total of $2500.\n\nOur Scholarship Presentation Dance for the past six years total approximately $2,000 each year, making a total of about $12,000.\n\nOur budget committee agrees that we have about 25 dependable women who have shared the responsibility of paying up to this point over $22,000 in an attempt to carry out our efforts toward promoting youth in accordance with the local and national structure. Personal loans, personal donations, and gifts — co-signing for loans have been excluded.\n\nThus, a big bouquet of roses to the loyal women in Sigma Sigma who have remained loyal to the chapter. Two charter members remain; they are Sorors Leslie B. Crossland and Ollie V. Greene.\n\nNews Coverage\n\nI wish to express deep appreciation to our Los Angeles Times for adequate and factual coverage of the Carver Project and the Negro History Week Project.\n\nBelow is the exact copy from Times of February 11, 1966. Our grateful thanks for Negro History Week efforts go to Soror Vassie Wright.\n\nNEGRO HISTORY WEEK\n\nFor 18 years, each time with added significance, Negro History Week has been observed in the United States. It is underway now, in the anniversary month of Abraham Lincoln and Frederick Douglass.\n\nIn public exercises throughout the Los Angeles area, the salient facts of American history as influenced by Negroes will be stressed. This is a worthwhile, even necessary, endeavor.\n\nThe Association for the Study of Negro Life and History deserves commendation for its dedicated effort to chronicle the role of an ethnic group that has done much and is doing more to make our country great.","excerptLabel":"From the page","scene":"Sigma Sigma''s page: adult education at Manual Arts School, Soror Catherine Stiefel''s tribute to the chapter''s scholarship giving — over $22,000 across its first twenty-five years — and the Los Angeles Times editorial on Negro History Week, reprinted.","thread":"“Leslie B. Crossland,” as this page prints her name.","threadLabel":"As printed","source":"article","line":"Sigma Sigma''s page: adult education, the chapter''s scholarship tribute, and the Times editorial.","alt":"The Aurora, Summer 1966, page 13: the Sigma Sigma chapter page, Los Angeles"}}'::jsonb)
on conflict (key) do update set value = excluded.value, updated_at = now();

insert into public.museum_content (key, value) values ('exhibit:aurora-13', '{"type":"exhibit","area":"aurora","order":12,"narration":"data:audio/mpeg;base64,","data":{"year":"1966","file":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","aspect":0.7896,"paper":"The Aurora","city":"Los Angeles, California","date":"Summer 1966","page":"Page 14","metaHTML":"<b>The Aurora</b> · Los Angeles, California<br/>Summer 1966 · Page 14","headline":"Negro History Week in Los Angeles","deck":"","excerpt":"Mayor Samuel W. Yorty issues Proclamation for Negro History Week, February 12-20, 1966, to Soror Vassie D. Wright, President-Founder of Our Authors Study Club. (L-r) Mr. Jesse Overstreet, Co-chairman and Mortician; Soror Wright, President; Mayor Yorty; and Mrs. Leontyne King, Library Commissioner.\n\nNegro History Week in Los Angeles\n\nPROCLAMATION\n\nWHEREAS, Our Authors Study Club, the Los Angeles Branch of the Association for the Study of Negro Life and History has been the official sponsor of annual city-wide Negro History Week celebrations since 1949; and\n\nWHEREAS, this program has contributed to the understanding of the Negro''s role in American history and in the life of our city, state and country, contributing to better race relations and giving opportunity to all citizens to become better informed as to the Negro''s contributions to education, science, the arts and other fields of endeavor; and\n\nWHEREAS, the Negro History Week celebration in Los Angeles, through our Authors Study Club, is a rededication to the principles of freedom and equality, highlighted by ceremonies to be held Monday, February 14 at 12 noon, on the steps of the Los Angeles City Hall:\n\nNOW, THEREFORE, I, Sam Yorty, Mayor of the City of Los Angeles, do hereby proclaim the week beginning Sunday, February 13, 1966, as Negro History Week in the City of Los Angeles, urging all citizens of our great City to join with the dedicated members of our Authors Study Club in attending the City Hall ceremonies and other events emphasizing the role of the Negro in our own community as a creative force in the history of our great Nation.\n\nGovernor Brown''s Message\n\nThe observance of Negro History Week, February 12-20, reminds us again of the struggle of these Americans to become first-class citizens.\n\nAt this time we have an opportunity to recall and reemphasize the contributions which the Negro people have made to the social, economic and cultural development of the United States. It is a time for Californians to renew and enrich their determination to make first-class citizenship a reality for all our people.\n\nThe history of mankind tells us that we cannot survive in a society that is not free. The history of our country has evolved from a dream that all men would live together in harmony and goodwill. And the history of the Negro people in this nation is a reflection of the hope for freedom in our society.\n\nAt this 1966 observance of Negro History Work, let us dedicate ourselves to those fundamental beliefs.","excerptLabel":"From the page","scene":"Mayor Samuel W. Yorty presents the Negro History Week proclamation to Soror Vassie D. Wright, President-Founder of Our Authors Study Club, with Governor Brown''s message for the February 12–20, 1966 observance in Los Angeles.","thread":"“Negro History Work,” as the Governor''s message prints it.","threadLabel":"As printed","source":"article","line":"Mayor Yorty''s Negro History Week proclamation, presented to Soror Vassie D. Wright.","alt":"The Aurora, Summer 1966, page 14: Mayor Yorty presents the Negro History Week proclamation to Soror Vassie D. Wright"}}'::jsonb)
on conflict (key) do update set value = excluded.value, updated_at = now();

insert into public.museum_content (key, value) values ('exhibit:aurora-14', '{"type":"exhibit","area":"aurora","order":13,"narration":"data:audio/mpeg;base64,","data":{"year":"1966","file":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","aspect":0.264,"paper":"The Aurora","city":"Los Angeles, California","date":"Summer 1966","page":"Pages 15–17","metaHTML":"<b>The Aurora</b> · Los Angeles, California<br/>Summer 1966 · Pages 15–17","headline":"Negro History Week News","deck":"","excerpt":"Negro History Week News\n\nSoror Vassie D. Wright, Founder-President of Our Authors Study Club, the Los Angeles Branch of the Association for the study of Negro Life and History, sponsored Negro History Week in Los Angeles, Feb. 12-20, 1966. Theme: “Freedom from Racial Myths and Stereotypes Through Negro History.”\n\nAn outstanding program was presented each night, opening with a Youth Work Shop, sponsored by the Beta Theta Chapter of Phi Delta Kappa Sorority. Over six hundred young people were in attendance. February 1, the Mass Meeting was held at Trinity Baptist Church. Our guest speaker was Dr. Daniel W. Wynn, Associate Director, Department of Educational Institutions, Division of Higher Education for the Methodist Church, Nashville, Tenn. He formerly served as Chaplain at Tuskegee Institute. An Art Exhibit and Reception followed at the Wilfandel Club House, sponsored by Delta Sigma Theta Sorority.\n\nMonday, February 14, at 12 noon there was a Negro History Queen ceremony on the steps of the City Hall. Mayor Samuel W. Yorty gave the official welcome. He also issued the proclamation urging all citizens to observe Negro History Week. Following this program Mayor and Mrs. Samuel W. Yorty gave a reception honoring the President, Soror Wright, and the members of Our Authors Study Club.\n\nAmong the other events during the week was the Youth Rally, held Saturday, at Hollywood High School; over one thousand Senior High students were present. Stanley Sanders, the Rhodes Scholar and Yale law student, was guest speaker. Sunday, there was the crowning of “Miss Negro History Week” and citations were awarded to outstanding citizens in various fields. Eight students participated in the oratorical contest held at Manual Arts High School. There were four winners who received scholarships. Sigma Sigma Sorors were very active in this year''s celebration of Negro History Week; Soror Tiny Nettles served on the Citation and hostess committees; Soror Minnette Fines on the Banquet committee. Others in attendance were Sorors Frankie Johnson, Bernice Everest, Juanita Massey, Madelene Dane, and Veking Tollette, Basileus, who made the presentation for the Sorority, at the Testimonial Banquet, to Soror Vassie Wright. All sorors in Sigma Sigma cooperated to make Negro History Week a success.\n\nSoror Veking Tollette makes presentation to Soror Vassie Davis Wright on behalf of Sigma Sigma Chapter at a Testimonial Banquet held Friday, February 18, at the Statler-Hilton Hotel.\n\nYouth Emphasis in Alpha Nu and Sigma Sigma Chapters\n\nTeam III Leaders stress togetherness as they work on the Chapters'' fund-raising plan to supplement the budget through a Rhomania Contest. Graduate Sorors, Alpha Nu and parents are working on the team, with the guidance of the three leaders—Sorors Claudia E. Whitmore, Tina Nettles, and Ollie V. Greene.\n\nOn June 25, 1966, at the Biltmore Hotel on the eve of the close of our 14th Regional Conference, a newly initiated young Sigma will be crowned Queen at the Annual Scholarship Presentation Ball. The awarding of scholarships will also be a part of the evening''s program when the Spotlight will be focused on the younger set.\n\nBeta Theta Sigma — Berkeley, California\n\nSoror Frankie Johnson (second from right), Reading Specialist at 112th Street School, recently participated in a ceremony in which Supervisor Kenneth Hahn (center) presented a resolution to Sigma Gamma Rho Sorority members for their participation in the George Washington Carver Exhibit at the Museum of Science and Industry during Negro History Week. Soror Johnson was co-chairman of publicity. (Below) Approach—Teamwork. Seated are Renee Dean, Pat Harrison, and Hazel Smalley, three contestants among the competitors for the “Crown,” with their leaders, Sorors Whitmore, Nettles, and Greene.\n\nOur Chapter was represented at the Neighborhood meeting.\n\nDuring Sigma Month we took for one of our projects “Words of Cheer to the Less fortunate,” and we were made happy. We made a special visit to a Rest Home and carried what we thought they might enjoy, and did. They were happy someone cared along with their families.\n\nAt Stockton, Soror Myrtle Neal put out the Welcome Mat again, and again we went on a motorcade from Berkeley. A lovely menu was served. The “welcome back” to Soror Ann Stedman after her illness made us feel like real sisters; then, the lovely reports from Soror Rose Boone concerning the undergraduates made us feel we had done nothing and received so much in return. Soror Neal presented gifts to Ann and Soror Alma J. Gordon — our “Senior Soror,” who is “just so happy to be that and ''kinder'' useful to our Chapter,” she said. A card was read from Soror Ollie V. Greene about a Carver Club.\n\nSoror Virginia Sims, Regional Syntaktes, was introduced and outlined her program for the Region. Several plan to be in attendance at the Regional Conference.\n\nSoror Gordon will attend the 7th Assembly of the Woman''s Society of Christian Service and the Wesleyan Service Guild in Portland, Oregon, May 12-15, along with people from all over the world.\n\nSoror Audrey Fitzhugh Barr — Beta Rho Sigma\n\nSoror Audrey Fitzhugh Barr is the newest member of Beta Rho Sigma Chapter, Denver, Colorado.\n\nSoror Barr is a graduate of LeMoyne College, Memphis, Tennessee, and is employed as a Public School teacher at Maria Mitchell Elementary School, Denver, Colorado. She has taught in Japan and in Germany and has served as secretary of the Fort Carson, Colorado, P.T.A.\n\nAn active member of the New Hope Baptist Church of Denver, Soror Barr is pianist for the Adult Department of that Sunday School, and finds time to accompany the Junior Choir of the Good Shepherd Baptist Church. She is also a music teacher.\n\n(continued from page 6)\n\nsolidly, we stand behind them. We viewed the fight to the fight in Little Rock 1956 to Selma 1965. We watched and took part in the Freedom March in Washington, D.C. We have seen the young people we have reared impatient with intolerance, racial prejudices and discrimination. We have heard their cries for freedom, now and understand their impatience. We who silently accepted situations, as they existed, hoping and praying for a brighter future — today we see our youth demand what is rightfully and legally theirs — freedom, total freedom. We are freer today than we were yesterday but we are not yet free. The longer the Negro waits for equality the further back he seems to slip into society. Young people quickly recognize this fact and so they say the time is now.\n\nClosely examining Sigma''s past history we find likewise forty-four years ago Sigma Gamma Rho was born when seven women established the first Chapter in Indiana.\n\nThese women serve as a source of strength, guidance and leadership for women today. Were it not for their efforts, their foresight, where would Sigma be now? These first sorors are deserving of honor, recognition, and gratitude; and their place in the sun is established in the annals of Sigma. When times are perplexed, when effort seems lost, when the fight is all done, where may we turn? Here, to these sorors from whence inspiration may be gathered. For it was through their initiative, unselfishness, and untiring works, that our sorority has achieved phenomenal growth. From seven (7) to over seven thousand (7,000) sorors. What we are today or will be tomorrow, may be credited to what those sorors did yesterday.\n\nFour decades ago the beacon light beamed forth illuminating the way, showing the need for a sorority such as ours. The pioneering sorors possessed the stamina to withstand adversity, blaze a new trail, build a society of women to render benefits for mankind. Our founders were deeply sensitive to the responsibilities intelligent women owed to our youth. We, who have been so blessed with an abundance of opportunities, should give thanks and be grateful by returning blessings upon others and bestowing love toward our sisters. These sorors have given valiant service in advancing the work\n\n(continued on page 26)\n\nWHICH BOULE???\n\nNEXT BOULE DETROIT, MICHIGAN, AUGUST 9-11, 1967","excerptLabel":"From the pages","scene":"Western Region chapter news across three pages of the Aurora: Negro History Week in Los Angeles, youth emphasis in Alpha Nu and Sigma Sigma, Beta Theta Sigma of Berkeley, and new Beta Rho Sigma member Audrey Fitzhugh Barr.","thread":"","threadLabel":"","source":"article","line":"Western Region chapter news: Negro History Week, youth emphasis, Beta Theta Sigma, and a new Beta Rho Sigma member.","alt":"The Aurora, Summer 1966, pages 15–17: Western Region chapter news","img2":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","alt2":"Soror Veking Tollette presenting to Soror Vassie Davis Wright at the February 18 Testimonial Banquet","cap2":"Soror Veking Tollette makes presentation to Soror Vassie Davis Wright on behalf of Sigma Sigma Chapter at a Testimonial Banquet held Friday, February 18, at the Statler-Hilton Hotel."}}'::jsonb)
on conflict (key) do update set value = excluded.value, updated_at = now();

insert into public.museum_content (key, value) values ('exhibit:aurora-15', '{"type":"exhibit","area":"aurora","order":14,"narration":"data:audio/mpeg;base64,","data":{"year":"1966","file":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","aspect":0.7684,"paper":"The Aurora","city":"Denver, Colorado","date":"Summer 1966","page":"Page 27","metaHTML":"<b>The Aurora</b> · Denver, Colorado<br/>Summer 1966 · Page 27","headline":"An Undergraduate Chapter in Denver","deck":"Gamma Omicron Chapter, organized May 8, 1966","excerpt":"Sigma Gamma Rho Sorority Establishes Undergraduate Chapter in Denver, Colorado\n\nThe Sorors of Beta Rho Sigma Chapter beamed with pride as its first local undergraduate chapter was established at Denver, Colorado.\n\nGamma Omicron Chapter of Sigma Gamma Rho Sorority, Inc., was organized May 8, 1966, by Soror Virginia Sims, Regional Syntaktes of the Western Region.\n\nThe ceremony and the installation were held at the home of Soror Ernestyne Henry, 1884 Gaylord Street.\n\nThe Charter was presented to the undergraduate advisor, Soror Mary Boston, who in turn presented it to members of Gamma Omicron Chapter.\n\nOfficers of Gamma Omicron Chapter are:\n\nBasileus — Soror Joan M. V. Henry\n\nAnti-Basileus — Soror Marva J. Peterson\n\nGrammateus — Soror Leona Reed\n\nAnti-Grammateus — Soror Rochelle Moore\n\nTamiochus — Soror Marietta Giddings\n\nEpistoleus — Soror Patricia Rose\n\nThe new sorors were showered with gifts—gold purses with blue symbols of Sigma Gamma Rho Sorority were given by members of Beta Rho Sigma, and travel kits consisting of folding toothbrushes, lotion dispensers, and toothpaste were given by Soror Sims.\n\nThe new Sorors, the Regional Syntaktes, and members of Beta Rho Sigma closed the day''s activities with a five course dinner at “Saltwater Dumas,” a local restaurant. They were later joined by members of their families and friends.\n\nGamma Omicron Chapter, Sorors Joan Henry, Marva Peterson, Marietta Giddings, Rochelle Moore, Leona Reed and Patricia Rose.\n\nOfficers of Beta Rho Sigma and Gamma Omicron Chapters. Seated (l-r) Sorors Marva Peterson, Marietta Giddings, Rochelle Moore and Leona Reed; standing, Ernestyne Henry, Syntaktes Virginia Sims, Verene Massey, Martha White, Beatrice Beaty and Mary Boston.","excerptLabel":"From the article","scene":"Gamma Omicron Chapter was organized in Denver on May 8, 1966, by Soror Virginia Sims, Regional Syntaktes of the Western Region. Beta Rho Sigma, the graduate chapter, presented the charter, and the day closed with a five-course dinner.","thread":"","threadLabel":"","source":"article","line":"Gamma Omicron undergraduate chapter organized in Denver, May 8, 1966.","alt":"The Aurora, Summer 1966, page 27: Gamma Omicron Chapter organized in Denver, with photographs"}}'::jsonb)
on conflict (key) do update set value = excluded.value, updated_at = now();
